mc(4, 'epidermal', 'Which statement is true for the epidermis of the root?', 'It lacks a cuticle and has root hairs', ['It has a thick cuticle and stomata', 'It has no cells', 'It has many chloroplasts'], 'Root epidermis is for absorption.');
mc(4, 'root', 'Which of the following is true for a dicot root but NOT a monocot root?', 'Secondary growth occurs', ['Presence of endodermis', 'Presence of pericycle', 'Exarch xylem'], 'Both have endodermis, pericycle and exarch xylem. Only dicot roots develop secondary growth.');
mc(4, 'root', 'The xylem in roots is described as exarch, meaning', 'protoxylem lies toward the periphery', ['protoxylem lies toward the centre', 'there is no protoxylem', 'there is no metaxylem'], 'Metaxylem towards the centre and protoxylem towards the periphery.', 'Endarch is the stem condition.');
mc(4, 'stem', 'Which of these is true for a dicot stem but not a monocot stem?', 'Medullary rays', ['Conjoint bundles', 'Endarch xylem', 'Water-containing cavities in bundles'], 'Medullary rays occur between bundles in dicots. Both have conjoint bundles and endarch xylem; water cavities are a monocot feature.');
mc(4, 'stem', 'The water cavity of the monocot vascular bundle', 'is formed by the breakdown of protoxylem elements', ['is formed by cambium', 'is in the pith', 'is in the cork'], 'The cavity is a water-containing space in the monocot bundle.');
mc(4, 'leaf', 'Which of the following is true for the upper epidermis of a dorsiventral leaf?', 'It has fewer stomata than the lower epidermis', ['It has more stomata', 'It has no cuticle', 'It has spongy cells'], 'Stomata are generally more on the lower (abaxial) epidermis.');
mc(4, 'leaf', 'The bulliform cells of grass leaves', 'help in rolling the leaf under water stress', ['carry out photosynthesis', 'conduct sugars', 'form guard cells'], 'These are large, empty, colourless cells in the upper epidermis.');
mc(4, 'secondary', 'The cork cambium usually arises in the', 'cortex', ['pith', 'xylem', 'pericycle of a dicot root only'], 'In stems it develops from the cortex region.');
mc(4, 'secondary', 'Sapwood differs from heartwood in being', 'lighter and involved in conduction', ['darker and non-conducting', 'composed only of dead cork', 'always outside the bark'], 'Sapwood is the peripheral secondary xylem.');
mc(4, 'secondary', 'The ring-like appearance of the wood in a cross-section is formed because', 'early wood and late wood differ in density and colour', ['the cork is in rings', 'phloem forms rings', 'tracheids are in rings only in the roots'], 'The seasonal change in cambial activity produces the pattern.');
mc(4, 'tissue systems', 'The vascular bundle with xylem and phloem on the same radius is', 'conjoint', ['radial', 'alternate', 'diffuse only in roots'], 'Conjoint bundles are typical of stems and leaves.');
mc(4, 'tissue systems', 'Radial vascular bundles are typical of', 'roots', ['stems', 'leaves', 'bark'], 'Xylem and phloem lie on different radii.');
mc(4, 'tissue systems', 'Which of the following tissue systems includes cortex, pericycle and pith?', 'Ground tissue system', ['Epidermal system', 'Vascular system', 'Meristematic system'], 'Ground tissue consists of all tissues except the epidermis and the vascular bundles.');
mc(4, 'secondary', 'The vascular cambium within the vascular bundle is called', 'intrafascicular cambium', ['interfascicular cambium', 'cork cambium', 'phelloderm'], 'The cambium in the medullary rays between bundles is interfascicular cambium.');
mc(4, 'simple', 'The fibres of coconut husk and the gritty pulp of pear both come from', 'sclerenchyma', ['collenchyma', 'parenchyma', 'phloem parenchyma'], 'Fibres and sclereids are the two types of sclerenchyma.');
mc(4, 'complex', 'Which pair contains only conducting elements of xylem?', 'Tracheids and vessels', ['Fibres and parenchyma', 'Sieve tubes and vessels', 'Companion cells and tracheids'], 'The two conducting cells of xylem.');
